WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:03.389
Let's talk about taboos.

00:00:03.389 --> 00:00:09.320
People writing in and saying, should politics
and religion be taboo in conversation with

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:15.690
significant others, with family, with your
significant others, family at work, et cetera?

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:21.419
So the way I grew up was politics and religion
are just like any other topics.

00:00:21.419 --> 00:00:26.470
You talk about them with your family, your
immediate family at larger family gatherings,

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:27.910
with friends, etc.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:29.869
Sometimes there are disagreements, sometimes
there are not.

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:36.200
But assuming nobody is espousing some truly
violent, disgusting position, you talk about

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.730
politics and religion just like you do anything
else.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:43.100
And then everybody still friends and everybody
is still respectful and the like.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:49.860
I actually now have an even stronger position
on this because online discourse and social

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:54.600
media expose people to political discussion.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.760
That is the worst you can imagine.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:02.990
I actually think it would be particularly
beneficial for kids from a young age to be

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:09.590
exposed to discussions about politics and
religion that are respectful and that don't

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:12.149
devolve into personal attacks.

00:01:12.149 --> 00:01:17.460
Because unfortunately, if at home politics
and religion aren't being discussed, if it's

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:21.630
school politics and religion aren't being
discussed, if wherever else they're not being

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:22.630
discussed.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.710
And the model people have is from.

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:27.039
Twitter, we're really, really screwed.

00:01:27.039 --> 00:01:31.899
And people end up not knowing how to peacefully
disagree with each other, how to respectfully

00:01:31.899 --> 00:01:34.200
express opinions that aren't the same.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.439
And then just to go back to living with each
other or working with each other, every disagreement

00:01:38.439 --> 00:01:40.939
is seen as a personal attack.

00:01:40.939 --> 00:01:43.140
People often become triggered.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:44.479
It's not good.

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:48.810
And we all benefit from talking about our
views and we benefit from having them challenged.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.250
And we benefit from not having topics be off
limits.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:55.959
Now, this doesn't mean you ignore decorum
or politeness or or don't use common sense.

00:01:55.959 --> 00:01:56.959
Right.

00:01:56.959 --> 00:02:01.219
If you're on a first date, maybe you do talk
about politics or maybe you don't.

00:02:01.219 --> 00:02:05.909
Now, very often politics and religion can
be the difference maker, whether two people

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:06.909
are compatible.

00:02:06.909 --> 00:02:08.810
So maybe you do want to talk about it right
away.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:10.360
Maybe you leave it for the second or the third
date.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:15.010
I don't know if it's your first time meeting
your significant others parents.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.390
Maybe you skip politics and religion.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:22.770
That one time, not because you aren't comfortable
discussing it respectfully with whoever.

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:28.709
But simply for for decorum and to avoid disagreements
in the interests of establishing what hopefully

00:02:28.709 --> 00:02:30.000
will be a long term relationship.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.000
So use your judgment.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.800
Don't you apply common sense?

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:39.180
I think there's one other element of this
that's important to mention.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:45.349
Not only are some topics taboo like politics
and religion, but we also have a big deficit

00:02:45.349 --> 00:02:51.540
of critical thinking skills and skills with
which we can examine our own beliefs and how

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.110
we came to our conclusions.

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:59.500
And I think that if we want to go in the direction
of removing the taboo around talking about

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.110
politics and religion where you're, quote,
not supposed to.

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:09.290
We do need people who are better informed
about how to apply critical thinking and basic

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:10.900
epistemology skills.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:15.610
Overall, though, I think we should be doing
more discussion of topics that often become

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:18.010
emotionally charged, not less.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:23.400
And there has been no more important time
to be talking about politics and religion

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.600
and subjects that can be difficult in all
scenarios.

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:29.019
Of course, again, applying common sense.

00:03:29.019 --> 00:03:30.629
If you disagree, let me know.

00:03:30.629 --> 00:03:32.319
We've got a great bonus show for you today.

00:03:32.319 --> 00:03:37.809
